---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/paths';
---

<Base
  title="Harness-Backed Agent Session Manager - Forager"
  description="Run Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, and other harness-backed agent sessions with tmux persistence, git worktrees, status detection, and local review surfaces."
>
  <Nav />

  <article class="pt-32 pb-16 px-6">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose-content">
      <header class="mb-12">
        <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-white mb-6 leading-tight">Harness-Backed Agent Session Manager</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
          Forager lists coding CLI sessions, attaches to their tmux terminals and manages optional Git worktrees. Use it with installed tools such as Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, OpenCode and Mistral Vibe.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section>
        <h2>What Is an AI Coding Agent</h2>
        <p>
          An AI coding agent is a CLI tool that reads your codebase, writes code, runs commands, and iterates on the results. Unlike a chat interface where you copy and paste snippets, a coding agent operates directly in your project directory. It can edit files, run tests, install dependencies, and commit changes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Examples include Claude Code (Anthropic), Codex (OpenAI), Gemini CLI (Google), OpenCode, and Mistral Vibe. They all share the same basic workflow: you give the agent a task in a terminal, it works through the problem, and you review the results. The differences are in the underlying model, tool use, and interaction style, but the operational needs are the same.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Running Multiple Agent Instances</h2>
        <p>
          Parallel sessions can handle separate tasks, such as refactoring a module and writing tests. Give changes separate working directories and review how they interact before merging them.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={sitePath('/')}>Forager</a> handles this by creating tmux sessions for each agent instance. Each session can point at a different directory or branch. The TUI dashboard shows all your instances at a glance, regardless of which coding agent you are using.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Status Detection Across Agents</h2>
        <p>
          Forager estimates session state from process and terminal patterns for Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, OpenCode and Mistral Vibe. The dashboard uses labels such as:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Running</strong>: Activity patterns suggest the agent is working</li>
          <li><strong>Waiting</strong>: A prompt or approval pattern was detected</li>
          <li><strong>Idle</strong>: No recognized activity or waiting pattern was detected</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Labels help you choose a session to inspect. They can be stale or misclassified and do not establish that a task finished successfully.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Git Worktrees for Parallel Sessions</h2>
        <p>
          With worktree mode enabled, <a href={sitePath('/guides/git-worktrees-ai-development/')}>git worktrees</a> give sessions separate branches and working directories. They share Git history and do not restrict an agent's access to other files or processes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Separate checkouts reduce accidental edits to the same working files. Review overlapping changes and shared resources before merging results.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Session Persistence</h2>
        <p>
          Agent sessions run inside tmux and can continue after you close the Forager TUI or disconnect its client. Reattach while the host and tmux server stay running. A reboot or tmux server exit ends the live processes.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is especially useful for long-running tasks. Start an agent on a large refactoring job, close the TUI, and come back later to check the results.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Getting Started</h2>
        <p>
          Install Forager and add your first coding agent session:
        </p>
        <pre><code>curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kimyoungjin06/forager-cli/main/scripts/install.sh | bash
forager</code></pre>
        <p>
          The TUI will guide you through creating sessions. It works with whichever coding agent you prefer. See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/')}>documentation</a> for the full setup guide. Related guides:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/docs/hosted-harness-agents.html')}>Hosted harness agents</a></li>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/guides/git-worktrees-ai-development/')}>Git worktrees for parallel development</a></li>
          <li><a href={sitePath('/guides/tmux-ai-coding-workflow/')}>tmux workflow for AI coding</a></li>
        </ul>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <Footer />
</Base>
